  "content": {
    "id": "p12-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p12-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Suppose that $\\alpha+\\beta=180^\\circ$. Prove that $\\cos\\alpha+\\cos\\beta=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\cos\\alpha+\\cos\\beta=0$.\n\nUse $\\cos(180^\\circ-\\beta)=-\\cos\\beta$.",
          "worked": "Since $\\alpha=180^\\circ-\\beta$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\alpha&=\\cos(180^\\circ-\\beta)\\\\\\cos\\alpha&=-\\cos\\beta\\\\\\cos\\alpha+\\cos\\beta&=0.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of length $a$, $b$ and $c$. Let $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$. Using the cosine rule, prove that\n$$2(a^2+b^2)=c^2+4m^2.$$\nThis is called Apollonius’ Theorem.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [
          {
            "id": "p12-q11-b-diagram",
            "role": "question",
            "format": "tikz",
            "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"geometry\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"source page 12 p12-q11-b-diagram\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,line cap=round,line join=round]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\coordinate (A) at (0,0);\n\\coordinate (B) at (4.8,0);\n\\coordinate (M) at (2.4,0);\n\\coordinate (C) at (1.65,2.9);\n\\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (A)--(C)--(B)--cycle;\n\\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (C)--(M);\n\\path (A)--(C) node[midway,left,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont] {$b$};\n\\path (B)--(C) node[midway,right,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont] {$a$};\n\\path (C)--(M) node[midway,right,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont] {$m$};\n\\path (A)--(M) node[midway,below,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont] {$\\frac c2$};\n\\path (M)--(B) node[midway,below,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont] {$\\frac c2$};\n\\node[left,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont] at (A) {$A$};\n\\node[right,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont] at (B) {$B$};\n\\node[above,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont] at (C) {$C$};\n\\node[below,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont] at (M) {$M$};\n\\pic[draw=none,angle radius=7mm,angle eccentricity=1,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont,\"$\\alpha$\"] {angle=C--M--A};\n\\pic[draw=none,angle radius=7mm,angle eccentricity=1,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont,\"$\\beta$\"] {angle=B--M--C};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
            "src": null,
            "widthMm": 59.6,
            "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
            "overlayOf": null,
            "transparent": false,
            "axes": null,
            "spec": {
              "sourcePage": 12,
              "sourceRegion": "Upper right, continuation of question 11(b)",
              "description": "Unshaded triangle with A–M–B horizontal and C above and slightly left of M. Angle labels have no added arcs.",
              "mathematics": "AC=b, BC=a, AB=c, AM=MB=c/2, CM=m, angle AMC=alpha, angle CMB=beta.",
              "answerVisibility": "All labels are permanent student-visible givens."
            },
            "derived": false,
            "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
          }
        ],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2(a^2+b^2)=c^2+4m^2$.\n\nAdd the cosine-rule equations for the two triangles and use $\\cos\\alpha+\\cos\\beta=0$.",
          "worked": "The cosine rule in $\\triangle AMC$ and $\\triangle BMC$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}b^2&=\\frac{c^2}{4}+m^2-cm\\cos\\alpha\\\\a^2&=\\frac{c^2}{4}+m^2-cm\\cos\\beta.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $A$, $M$ and $B$ are collinear, $\\alpha+\\beta=180^\\circ$. By part (a), $\\cos\\alpha+\\cos\\beta=0$. Adding the equations,\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2+b^2&=\\frac{c^2}{2}+2m^2-cm(\\cos\\alpha+\\cos\\beta)\\\\a^2+b^2&=\\frac{c^2}{2}+2m^2\\\\2(a^2+b^2)&=c^2+4m^2.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q11-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Now consider a parallelogram with sides of length $a$ and $b$. Suppose its diagonals are of length $c$ and $d$. Prove that\n$$2a^2+2b^2=c^2+d^2.$$\nThis result is called the Parallelogram Law. Hint: Use part (b).",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2a^2+2b^2=c^2+d^2$.\n\nThe diagonals bisect each other, so apply part (b) with median $m=\\frac{d}{2}$.",
          "worked": "Label the parallelogram $ACBD$, with $AC=BD=b$, $CB=DA=a$, $AB=c$ and $CD=d$. Let the diagonals meet at $M$.\n\nThe diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so $M$ is the midpoint of $AB$ and $CM=\\frac{d}{2}$. Applying part (b) to $\\triangle ACB$,\n$$\\begin{align*}2(a^2+b^2)&=c^2+4\\left(\\frac d2\\right)^2\\\\2(a^2+b^2)&=c^2+d^2\\\\2a^2+2b^2&=c^2+d^2.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
